/-
  When a parabolic homomorphism is determined by its values.

  `Period.parabolicHoms R Γ R` is the submodule of `Additive Γ →+ R` killed by the
  trace-`±2` (parabolic) elements of `Γ` — the lattice the period map of a cusp form
  measures against. This module collects the three *determination* statements about
  it, all for a finite-index `Γ ≤ SL(2, ℤ)`.

  * **The `±1` extension.** `Period.existsUnique_isParabolicHom_sup_zpowers_neg_one_apply_eq`:
    a parabolic hom on `Γ` extends uniquely to `Γ ⊔ ⟨-1⟩`. Since `-1` is central and
    hyperbolic-inverse (`trace = -2`, so its square of the trace is `4`), any parabolic
    hom kills it, and `Γ ⊔ ⟨-1⟩` is `Γ` together with the coset `-Γ`: every element of
    the extension is `±γ`. A parabolic hom is therefore constant on `±γ`, and that
    constancy is exactly what makes the naive formula a homomorphism.

  * **Determination by torsion.** `Period.exists_parabolicHoms_int_castAddHom_comp_eq_of_forall_isOfFinOrder`:
    a parabolic hom `Γ → ZMod n` that vanishes on every torsion element of `Γ` is the
    reduction mod `n` of a parabolic hom `Γ → ℤ`. The proof factors `SL(2, ℤ)` as the
    free product `(ZMod 2) ∗ (ZMod 3)` modulo its centre (`exists_mulEquiv_freeProduct_quotient_center`),
    so torsion is generated by the conjugates of `T`; a crossed homomorphism on the free
    product is a coboundary once its evaluation on the Bass–Serre generators vanishes,
    and the sum of the values over the `⟨T⟩`-orbits is `0` because each `ZMod n`-value is
    the reduction of an integer `χ`-value summed over a finite set of torsion elements.

  * **Determination by an invariant function.** `ModularCurve.exists_exp_eq_of_invariant_ne_zero_isParabolicHom`:
    a nowhere-zero `Γ`-invariant `Φ` with non-zero cusp limits at every cusp is `exp` of a
    primitive `L` whose period map `m : Γ → ℤ` is parabolic. `Φ` has a global logarithm
    (`exp` is a covering map), the coboundary `L (γ • τ) - L τ` is a locally constant
    `2πiℤ`-valued function on the connected `ℍ`, hence a constant `2πi m γ`, and `m` is
    parabolic because along a parabolic `γ` the primitive is bounded by the cusp limits.

  Everything is transcribed from the pin (`aa2d8b3`) — statement text from the
  `Theorems/` wrappers, proof text from the `P2M/Sol/S_` files. Every helper is
  `private`, so `spec/check_port.py` diffs only the three headlines.

  spec-pin: Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_Period_existsUnique_isParabolicHom_sup_zpowers_neg_one_apply_eq.lean
  spec-pin: P2M/Sol/S_ModularCurve_Period_existsUnique_isParabolicHom_sup_zpowers_neg_one_apply_eq.lean
  spec-pin: Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_Period_exists_parabolicHoms_int_castAddHom_comp_eq_of_forall_isOfFinOrder.lean
  spec-pin: P2M/Sol/S_ModularCurve_Period_exists_parabolicHoms_int_castAddHom_comp_eq_of_forall_isOfFinOrder.lean
  spec-pin: Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_exp_eq_of_invariant_ne_zero_isParabolicHom.lean
  spec-pin: P2M/Sol/S_ModularCurve_exists_exp_eq_of_invariant_ne_zero_isParabolicHom.lean
-/
